🔄 SwiftUI View Lifecycle Internals (Day 7)

SwiftUI does not follow UIKit lifecycle (viewDidLoad, viewWillAppear, etc.).

Instead, it uses a state-driven lifecycle.

Understanding this explains:

why body runs multiple times
when views are created/destroyed
why side effects can misfire
🧠 Core Principle
Views are ephemeral  
State is persistent

SwiftUI recreates views frequently, but preserves state using identity.

1️⃣ View Creation vs Persistence
UIKit
ViewController created once → lives in memory
SwiftUI
View struct recreated many times
State persists separately
Example
struct MyView: View {

    init() {
        print("init called")
    }

    var body: some View {
        Text("Hello")
    }
}

👉 init may run multiple times.

2️⃣ Why body Runs Frequently
var body: some View {
    print("body recomputed")
    return Text("Hello")
}

Triggers:

state change
parent update
environment change
Key Insight
Frequent body recomputation is NORMAL
3️⃣ Lifecycle Hooks in SwiftUI

Instead of UIKit methods, SwiftUI provides:

.onAppear
.onAppear {
    print("appeared")
}

Called when the view becomes visible.

.onDisappear
.onDisappear {
    print("disappeared")
}

Called when removed from screen.

.task
.task {
    await loadData()
}

Lifecycle-aware async hook.

4️⃣ .onAppear vs .task
	.onAppear	.task
Async support	❌	✅
Cancellation	❌	✅
Lifecycle aware	Partial	Full
5️⃣ View Identity Controls Lifecycle
MyView()
.id(UUID())

This forces:

New identity → new lifecycle

Effects:

init called again
state resets
.onAppear triggers again
6️⃣ Parent-Child Lifecycle

When a parent updates:

ParentView {
    ChildView()
}

SwiftUI may:

Recreate ChildView even if unchanged

But state remains if identity is stable.

7️⃣ Common Lifecycle Bugs
❌ Bug 1 — API called multiple times
.onAppear {
    fetchData()
}

Cause:

View appears multiple times
✅ Fix
.task {
    await fetchData()
}
❌ Bug 2 — State resets unexpectedly

Cause:

Identity changed
❌ Bug 3 — Side effects in body
var body: some View {
    fetchData() // ❌
}
8️⃣ When Views Are Destroyed

SwiftUI destroys views when:

removed from hierarchy
navigation pops
identity changes
9️⃣ Debugging Lifecycle

Add logs:

init {
    print("init")
}

deinit {
    print("deinit")
}

And:

print("body")

This reveals lifecycle patterns.

🔥 Key Insight
SwiftUI lifecycle is driven by data, not events
🧠 Interview Power Answer

If asked:

“Why does SwiftUI call body multiple times?”

Answer:

“Because SwiftUI recalculates the view hierarchy whenever state changes. Since views are value types, this is cheap and expected.”

⭐ Golden Rules
Views are temporary
State is persistent
Avoid side effects in body
Use .task for async work
Identity controls lifecycle
